  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the equation of the line perpendicular to $y=4x-6$ that passes through the point $(8,-3).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Let ${l}_{1}$ denote the line $y=4x-6,$ and let ${l}_{2}$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of ${l}_{2}$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of ${l}_{1}.$ Since ${l}_{1}$ has a slope of $4,$ the line ${l}_{2}$ must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(8,-3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-3) & =-\\frac{1}{4}(x-8) \\\\ y+3 & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
